    category: "Root Canal Treatment",
    heroImageAlt: "A person gently touching their cheek near the jaw while checking for swelling in a mirror.",
    bodyHtml: `
<h2>The short answer</h2><p>A small amount of tenderness or mild puffiness around a tooth in the first day or two after a root canal can be a normal part of healing, especially if the tooth was infected beforehand. However, swelling that grows, spreads to the cheek or face, or comes with fever is not something to wait out, and should prompt a call to your dentist or urgent care. Knowing the difference helps you respond calmly and appropriately.</p><h2>Why some swelling can happen</h2><p>Root canal treatment removes infected or inflamed tissue from inside the tooth, and the body's healing response to both the original infection and the procedure can cause temporary inflammation in the surrounding tissues. When a tooth had an abscess or significant infection before treatment, it is more likely that some residual swelling or tenderness will take a few days to settle. This kind of minor, stable, slowly improving swelling is usually part of recovery. Rest, staying hydrated, and keeping your head slightly elevated when you sleep can help your body manage mild inflammation in those first days.</p><h2>What is likely normal</h2><ul><li>Mild tenderness of the gum right around the treated tooth</li><li>A slight puffiness that stays local and does not keep growing</li><li>Soreness that eases a little each day over the first several days</li><li>Discomfort that responds to recommended pain relief and cold compresses</li></ul><p>If your swelling fits this picture and is trending in the right direction, it is often manageable at home while you keep your follow-up appointment. Even so, it is always reasonable to check in with your dentist if you are unsure. Taking a mental note of how the area looks each morning makes it easier to tell whether things are settling or changing, and it gives your dental team useful information if you do need to call.</p><h2>What is concerning</h2><p>Contact your dental office promptly, or seek urgent care, if you notice:</p><ul><li>Swelling that increases rather than settles after the first day or two</li><li>Swelling spreading into the cheek, jaw, neck, or under the chin</li><li>Fever, chills, or feeling generally unwell</li><li>A bad taste, discharge, or pus near the tooth</li><li>Swelling that makes the area feel hot and increasingly painful</li></ul><p>These can indicate an infection that needs active management rather than time alone.</p><h2>When swelling is an emergency</h2><p>Certain signs point to a spreading infection that requires immediate attention: difficulty breathing or swallowing, swelling that is rapidly enlarging, swelling that reaches the eye, or a high fever with marked facial swelling. In these situations, do not wait for a routine appointment; seek urgent or emergency medical or dental care right away. Serious spreading infections are uncommon, but they are treated most safely when addressed early.</p><h3>Can I use a cold or warm compress?</h3><p>For mild, expected swelling, a cold compress on the cheek in short intervals can ease discomfort in the early days. Warm compresses are sometimes suggested later in healing, but if swelling is spreading or you are unsure, contact your dentist before relying on home measures.</p><h3>Will antibiotics make swelling go down?</h3><p>Only a dentist can decide whether antibiotics are appropriate, and they are not a substitute for needed dental treatment. If swelling suggests infection, the source usually needs to be addressed directly, so reach out rather than trying to manage it alone.</p><h3>How long should swelling take to resolve?</h3><p>Minor swelling often improves within a few days, easing a little at a time rather than all at once. Swelling that persists beyond that, plateaus without improving, or worsens at any point should be evaluated so the cause can be identified and addressed.</p><h2>Getting it checked</h2><p>If you are recovering from a root canal and have swelling you are not sure about, a quick assessment brings peace of mind. For urgent concerns in the Markham area, the <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/dental-services/dental-emergencies" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">dental emergencies page at Smile Dental Arts Centre</a> explains how to reach the office, and you can also book a general appointment with <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Smile Dental Arts Centre in Markham</a>, where root canal treatment is provided. When symptoms are severe or spreading quickly, seek urgent care without delay.</p><p class="article-disclaimer"><em>This article is general information and not a substitute for professional dental advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always speak with a licensed dentist about your specific situation.</em></p>
`,
