    bodyHtml: `
<p>Most people recover from a root canal within a few days of mild soreness, feel close to normal within about a week, and complete the process once the permanent crown or filling is placed, which may be a couple of weeks later. Recovery is a sequence of stages rather than a single moment, and knowing roughly what to expect at each point makes the experience far less stressful. Here is a general timeline, keeping in mind that healing varies from person to person.</p><h2>Treatment day: the first hours</h2><p>Right after the appointment, your lip, cheek, and tongue stay numb for roughly one to three hours. Wait until the freezing fully fades before eating so you do not accidentally bite yourself. As sensation returns, the tooth may begin to feel tender. Many people go back to ordinary activities the same day, since a standard root canal uses local anaesthetic rather than sedation, though a quieter evening is reasonable if you prefer.</p><h2>Days one to three: the tender phase</h2><p>This is usually when the tooth and surrounding area feel most sore, particularly when biting. The discomfort tends to be a dull ache rather than the sharp pain many people had beforehand. Over-the-counter pain relief often helps when your dentist confirms it is suitable for you, and soft foods plus chewing on the other side keep pressure off the tooth. For a tooth that was significantly infected, this phase can be a little more noticeable.</p><h2>Days three to seven: steady improvement</h2><p>By now most people notice the tooth settling, with soreness fading day by day. You can gradually return to a more normal diet as comfort allows, while still protecting any temporary filling by avoiding hard and sticky foods on that side. If discomfort is instead increasing during this window, or swelling or fever appears, that is a reason to contact your dentist rather than wait.</p><h2>Week two and beyond: the final restoration</h2><p>A root canal is not truly finished until the tooth has its permanent restoration. Back teeth especially often need a crown to protect them from fracture, and this is typically arranged within the weeks after treatment. Once the crown or permanent filling is in place and the tooth has fully settled, it should feel like a normal, functional tooth. Returning for this step on schedule is one of the most important parts of the whole timeline.</p><h2>What affects how fast you heal</h2><p>Several factors influence recovery speed, including how infected or inflamed the tooth was beforehand, which tooth was treated (molars can take a little longer to calm down), whether the bite needs a minor adjustment, and your overall health. Because of these variables, timelines are a guide, not a guarantee. Your own recovery may be quicker or a bit slower, and that can be perfectly normal. It helps to compare how you feel from one day to the next rather than against a fixed schedule, since the direction of change, steadily improving, is usually more telling than the exact day you reach each stage.</p><h3>When can I chew normally on that tooth?</h3><p>Light chewing often returns within days as soreness eases, but heavy chewing on the treated side is best avoided until the permanent restoration is placed, since a temporarily filled tooth is more fragile.</p><h3>Is some sensitivity weeks later normal?</h3><p>A treated tooth can feel slightly different for a little while as everything settles, and mild awareness when biting may linger briefly. Pain that returns, worsens, or comes with swelling is different and should be checked.</p><h3>Do I still need check-ups after it heals?</h3><p>Yes. Routine dental visits let your dentist confirm the tooth and restoration are doing well over time, which supports the tooth's long-term health.</p><h2>Finishing your treatment</h2><p>Completing the permanent restoration is what turns a successful root canal into a lasting result. If you need a general dentist in Markham to finish the process or review your recovery, Dr. Neda Kadivar and <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">the team at Smile Dental Arts Centre in Markham</a> provide root canal treatment and restorative care. You can also read more about <a href="https://www.smiledentalartscentre.com/dental-services/endodontics/root-canal-therapy" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">root canal therapy at Smile Dental Arts Centre</a> to know what to expect.</p><p class="article-disclaimer"><em>This article is general information and not a substitute for professional dental advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Always speak with a licensed dentist about your specific situation.</em></p>
`,
